#What is Docker, Why we use docker and how to implement it?
-----------------------------------------------------------
Docker is a containerization platform that allows us to package an application along with its runtime environment, dependencies, and configuration into a Docker image. That image can then be run as a container on different environments consistently.
In a Spring Boot application, normally we build the application as a JAR and need Java and other dependencies configured on the server. With Docker, we package the Spring Boot JAR along with the required Java runtime into a Docker image.
We then run that image as a container. This helps us avoid the "it works on my machine" problem because the same containerized application can run consistently across development, testing, and production environments.
Docker is also useful for deployment, isolation, scalability, and CI/CD pipelines.
How do you implement Docker in Spring Boot?
First, I build my Spring Boot application using Maven or Gradle, which generates a JAR file. Then I create a Dockerfile containing the Java runtime image, copy the JAR into the image, expose the application port, and specify the command to start the application.
FROM eclipse-temurin:21-jre
WORKDIR /app
COPY target/myapp.jar app.jar
EXPOSE 8080
ENTRYPOINT ["java", "-jar", "app.jar"]
Then I build the Docker image:
docker build -t my-spring-app:1.0 .
And run it using:
docker run -p 8080:8080 my-spring-app:1.0
Now the Spring Boot application is running inside a Docker container and can be accessed through port 8080.
What is Docker Compose
Docker Compose is used when our application requires multiple containers, such as Spring Boot, MySQL, Redis, or Kafka. Instead of starting each container manually, we define them in a docker-compose.yml file and start them together using docker compose up.
