What is a Pod
A Pod is the smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes. It provides an execution environment for one or more containers. In a typical microservices application, we usually run one application container inside a Pod. Kubernetes manages the Pods and provides features such as scaling, self-healing, and rolling updates.

#What is Jenkins
---------------
Jenkins is an open-source automation server used to implement CI/CD pipelines. It automatically builds, tests, and deploys applications whenever developers push code to a source-code repository such as Git.
In a Spring Boot microservices project, Jenkins can automate the complete process from Git push → testing → Maven build → Docker image creation → Docker registry → deployment.
For example, when a developer pushes code to Git, Jenkins gets triggered. It runs unit tests, executes mvn clean package to create the JAR/WAR, builds a Docker image, pushes that image to a Docker registry such as ECR or JFrog, and then can trigger the deployment to Kubernetes.

#What is Kubernates
Kubernetes is an open-source container orchestration platform used to deploy and manage containerized applications in production. In our microservices architecture, we build Docker images for each microservice and push them to a container registry. Kubernetes pulls those images and runs them as Pods. We use Kubernetes for automated deployment, scaling, load balancing, service discovery, self-healing, health checks, and rolling updates. It allows each microservice to be independently deployed and scaled based on traffic.
Jenkins → automates the pipeline
Docker → packages the application
Registry → stores the image
Kubernetes → runs and manages the containers in production